  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Michigan received $5.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal buying environment in Michigan includes 146,381 awards with an average award size of $35.4K, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state with $2.5B obligated, followed by the General Services Administration with $1.8B. Automobile Manufacturing leads industry spending at $1.7B, while Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing received $1.3B. Large prime vendors include GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. with $1.3B, FORD MOTOR CO with $640M, and GENERAL MOTORS LLC with $611M. Yearly context shows a decline from prior peaks: FY25 totaled $6.6B and FY24 totaled $8B, down from FY22 at $9B. The buyer and vendor concentrations suggest Michigan contracting remains centered on defense and automotive supply chains, with smaller but meaningful activity in services like Security Guards and Patrol Services at $101M and Engineering Services at $85.3M. The fiscal year to date totals are partial and represent obligations recorded so far in FY2026.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove Michigan's FY2026 federal contract total?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.5B, making defense the single largest driver of obligations. Automobile Manufacturing received $1.7B and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing received $1.3B, concentrating spending in vehicle and defense-related manufacturing. Large vendors such as GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. at $1.3B and major automakers amplified that concentration."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are contracting opportunities concentrated in Michigan?",
        "body": "Automobile Manufacturing and defense-related manufacturing together account for the largest dollar shares, with $1.7B and $1.3B respectively. The General Services Administration obligated $1.8B, showing significant non-defense federal buying. Service and technical sectors show smaller pools, for example Security Guards and Patrol Services at $101M and Engineering Services at $85.3M."
      }
    ]
  },
